			"55": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "TEXTURE MATH\n\nEach pixel holds a value.\nAs mentioned before, at the default 8-bit(int) setting, each pixel in a Texture2Dchannel is a 0 to 1 value. \nIn the Texture2Drgba type, each pixel holds a value for each channel.\n\nWhen we start thinking about pixels as numbers, we can also start to conceptualize how math and logic would work. \nApplying an Add node to a texture will add the addition value to each pixel.\nAs you can see in the first example, adding to a Texture2Drgba type will give you a Float4 addition as we can add to each channel separately. \nWhen we do the same to the Texture2Dchannel, we can only add a single value as there is only a single channel.\n\nNow that we are thinking of pixels as numbers, we can also deduct that we can do math operations with textures. In the second example we are multiplying the Test Card with the Wire logo.\n\nEach channel of each pixel gets multiplied with the same channel of the same pixel of the other texture. \nThis can be clearly seen by the fact that the transparency of the Wire logo remains intact.\nA transparent pixel has a value of 0,0,0,0.\nSo whatever channel we multiply with, it will end up with a value of 0."
					}
				},
