			"90": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "TEXTURE LOGIC\n\nContinuing with the same thought process as with texture math, we can do logic operations on the channel values. With logic nodes we can do comparisons between pixels.\n\nIn the first example we take the alpha channel from the Wire logo and check which pixels are larger than 0. \nWire returns a black and white texture where all the values larger than 0 are white(true) and all the values equal or smaller than 0 are black(false). \nThis method could be used to filter out semi-transparent pixels (removing anti alias artifacts) by filtering out all pixels below a given alpha value.\n\nIn the second example we want to get a mask of all the black pixels.\nFor this reason we test the Texture2Dchannel against a black color (which is a float4).\nWe start by getting rid of all the colors by converting to grayscale.\nNext, we compare the grayscale image to the black color.\nThis returns a black and white texture where all the black pixels are white (true) and all non-black pixels are black (false). We use this as a mask for our original texture.\n"
					}
				},
